EXAMPLES = [
("my friend didn't invite me to her birthday dinner and i'm kind of hurt. should i say something?",
"""Probably, as long as you say it as a question rather than a verdict. Being left out stings, and the sting tends to curdle into distance if you swallow it. But you don't yet know why it happened. It might have been a small table, a guest list someone else drew up, or a plain oversight. Something like "I noticed I wasn't at your birthday dinner, and I'll admit it stung a little. Did I miss something?" gives her room to explain without putting her on trial. Whatever she says, you'll know more than you do now, and the friendship will be on firmer ground than silence would leave it."""),

("i think i'm burned out at work. everything feels like a chore",
"""That sounds exhausting, and it's worth taking seriously rather than pushing through. Burnout usually grows from a mismatch that persists for a long time: more demands than resources, little control over your work, or effort that never seems to be recognized. Rest helps, but if you return to the same conditions, the feeling tends to return with you. So it's worth asking what, specifically, has changed or worn thin. Is it the workload, the kind of work, or the people around it? Some of those can be addressed with a frank conversation with your manager; some can't. In the meantime, protect the things outside work that restore you, and if the flatness starts to spread into the rest of your life, talking to a doctor or therapist is a sensible next step."""),

("my roommate never does the dishes and it's driving me crazy",
"""This is one of the oldest frictions of shared living, and it rarely improves through silence and pointed sighing. Pick a calm moment, not the minute you find a sink full of pans, and talk about the problem rather than their character: "The dishes keep piling up, and it's starting to get to me. Can we figure out a system?" Then agree on something concrete, such as washing your own dishes the same day, or alternating weeks, and write it down if that helps. Most people respond better to a clear arrangement than to vague resentment. If the agreement collapses after a fair try, you'll at least know it's a pattern, not a misunderstanding."""),

("how do i tell my parents i'm leaving law to become a teacher",
"""Tell them as a decision you've thought through, not a question you're hoping they'll approve. Explain what drew you to teaching and what you learned about yourself in law, and let them see that this isn't a whim. Anticipate their real worries, which are usually money and security, and answer them plainly: how you'll manage financially, how you'll train, what the next year looks like. Then give them time. Parents often react first to the surprise and only later to the substance, and the second conversation tends to go better than the first. Their concern, even when it comes out as doubt, is usually a form of love."""),

("i have a presentation tomorrow and i'm super nervous",
"""Nerves before a presentation are almost universal, and they show far less from the outside than they feel from the inside. Tonight, run through the talk out loud once or twice, standing up, paying special attention to your first minute; a confident start carries momentum into everything after it. Then stop rehearsing and get some sleep. Tomorrow, before you begin, take a few slow breaths, with the exhale longer than the inhale, which genuinely calms the body. And remember that your audience wants you to do well. They're not waiting for a mistake; they're waiting to learn something."""),

("should i take a job that pays more but sounds kind of boring",
"""It depends on how much more, and on what kind of boring. A substantial raise can change your life in real ways: less financial strain, more savings, more freedom later. But boredom compounds, and work that drains you for forty hours a week has a way of leaking into the rest of your life. Some questions that might help: would the extra money buy you something you genuinely want, like security or a goal you're saving for? Is the job dull, or just unfamiliar? And what would you learn there that might lead somewhere more interesting? Sometimes a few years of well-paid tedium is a sound investment; sometimes it's a slow way of becoming someone you'd rather not be."""),

("a coworker keeps taking credit for my work. what should i do",
"""Start by making your work visible in ways that don't depend on anyone else's honesty. Share progress in group channels and meetings, send brief summary emails after you finish something, and present your own work whenever you can. If it keeps happening, talk to the coworker directly and calmly: "In the meeting, the analysis was described as yours. I did most of that work, and I'd like us to be clear about who did what." Sometimes that's enough. If it continues, bring specifics to your manager, focusing on the facts and the effect on your work rather than on the other person's motives. A paper trail makes that conversation much easier."""),

("my best friend's dog just died. what do i say?",
"""Keep it simple and personal. Something like "I'm so sorry about Bear. He was such a good dog, and I know how much you loved him" does more than any carefully phrased condolence. If you have a memory of the dog, share it; people who are grieving a pet often feel that others think they shouldn't be so sad, and hearing that someone else noticed and cared helps. Avoid rushing to comfort, such as talk of getting another dog or of a better place. Then offer something concrete, like dinner or a walk later in the week, because grief tends to be loneliest a few days after everyone has stopped mentioning it."""),

("i keep comparing myself to people on instagram and feeling bad",
"""You're comparing your whole life, with its tired mornings and its unglamorous Tuesdays, against other people's carefully chosen highlights. That contest can't be won, because one side is edited and the other isn't. It may help to notice which accounts reliably leave you feeling smaller, and mute them; nobody needs to know. Try spending the minutes you'd scroll on something that makes you feel capable, however small. And when the comparison creeps in anyway, ask whether you actually want the thing you're envying, or just the feeling the photo implies. Often it's the latter, and that feeling can be found elsewhere."""),

("is it weird to go to the movies alone",
"""Not at all, and it's one of the quietly great pleasures available to anyone. You choose the film, the showtime, and the seat. There's no negotiating about snacks and no one to elbow during the good parts, and you can sit with the ending afterward without having to discuss it. Once the lights go down, everyone in a cinema is effectively alone anyway. Go to a weekday matinee the first time, when the theater is quiet. You may find it becomes a small ritual you look forward to."""),

("my partner and i keep fighting about chores",
"""Chore fights are rarely only about chores; they're usually about fairness, and about feeling unseen. Pick a calm time to talk, not in the middle of an argument, and start by listing everything it takes to run your household, including the invisible work like planning meals, remembering birthdays, and noticing that the soap is running out. Many couples discover that one person is carrying far more of that mental load than the other realized. Then divide the tasks by ownership, not by help: whoever owns laundry handles it from start to finish, without needing reminders. And agree to revisit the arrangement in a month, since the first version rarely works perfectly."""),

("i got rejected from a job i really really wanted",
"""I'm sorry. That's a real disappointment, especially when you could already picture yourself in the role. Let it sting for a day or two before you rush to make it mean anything. A rejection says much less about you than it feels like it does; hiring decisions hinge on internal candidates, budget shifts, and a dozen things nobody tells you. If you made it to the interviews, it's worth politely asking for feedback, since some companies will share it. And notice what wanting this job taught you about what you want, because that clarity will be useful for the next application."""),

("i snapped at my coworker today and i feel terrible. how do i apologize",
"""Apologize soon, briefly, and without excuses. Something like "I'm sorry I snapped at you earlier. You didn't deserve that, and I was out of line" is enough. You can mention what was going on if it helps them understand, but keep it to a sentence and don't let it become a justification. Then let them respond however they respond, and don't ask them to reassure you. The fact that you feel terrible is a good sign: it means you care about how you treat people. A clean apology usually restores more trust than the outburst cost."""),

("i feel kind of lonely lately even though i'm around people all day",
"""That kind of loneliness is common and often misunderstood. It isn't about how many people are nearby; it's about whether any of them really know you. You can spend a whole day in meetings and still go home feeling unseen. What usually helps is depth rather than more company: a longer conversation with one person, telling a friend something true about how you've been, or spending time with someone where there's nothing to perform. If you can, reach out to one person this week and suggest something unhurried. And if the feeling settles in and won't lift, it's worth talking to someone about it, a friend or a professional, since loneliness that lasts tends to quietly shrink the rest of life."""),

("should i move to a new city? i'm kind of stuck",
"""It might be the right move, but it's worth separating two things first: wanting a different place, and wanting a different life. A new city can give you new streets, new people, and a fresh start, but you'll bring yourself along, along with your habits and whatever is making you feel stuck. Ask what specifically you'd be moving toward. Is it a job, a community, a landscape, a cost of living? If you can name it, and the new place genuinely offers it, moving can be wonderfully clarifying. If you can't, try changing one smaller thing where you are first and see whether the stuck feeling shifts."""),

("my friend always cancels plans last minute",
"""Repeated last-minute cancellations usually mean something, though not necessarily what it feels like. Your friend may be overwhelmed, anxious, depressed, or simply bad at estimating their own energy. It's worth asking gently: "I've noticed our plans fall through a lot lately. Is everything okay?" That opens the door without accusation. You can also make plans that are harder to cancel and easier to keep: shorter, closer to home, or with tickets bought in advance. If the pattern continues regardless, it's fair to adjust how much you invest, and to save your bigger plans for people who show up."""),

("how do i set boundaries with my boss who messages me late at night",
"""Set the boundary through behavior first, then words if needed. Unless something is truly urgent, wait until the morning to reply, and do it promptly and well when you do. Most managers adjust quickly once they see that night messages get morning answers without any drop in quality. If it continues, raise it directly and frame it around doing good work: "I've found I do my best work when I fully switch off in the evenings. Unless something's urgent, is it all right if I respond to late messages first thing in the morning?" Asking what counts as urgent also helps, because it gives both of you a shared line to work with."""),

("i'm scared of choosing the wrong grad program",
"""That fear makes sense, because the decision feels permanent. It's less permanent than it seems, though. People change fields, transfer, and build careers that wander far from their degrees all the time. It may help to think less about choosing the perfect program and more about choosing a good next step. Which program puts you around people doing work you admire? Where would you learn the skills you'd want regardless of where you end up? Talk to current students, not just admissions, since they'll tell you what it's actually like. Then make a reasonable choice and commit to it fully. Most good outcomes come from what you do inside a choice rather than from the choice itself."""),

("my sister is marrying a guy i don't really like. should i tell her",
"""It depends on what you don't like about him. If you have a specific, serious concern, such as how he treats her, something dishonest, or anything that touches her safety, tell her privately and gently, once, focusing on what you've seen rather than on who he is. If it's more that he's not your kind of person, keep it to yourself. She's choosing a partner for her life, not yours, and a sibling who opposed the marriage can find themselves on the outside of it for years. Either way, make sure she knows you'll be there for her, whatever happens."""),

("how do i get over a breakup",
"""Slowly, and not in a straight line. In the early weeks, give yourself fewer chances to reopen the wound: mute or unfollow them, put away the things that ambush you, and resist the late-night message. Lean on friends more than feels polite; that's what they're for. Keep some structure to your days, such as meals, sleep, movement, and a walk outside, even when you don't feel like it. Then, gradually, start doing things that belong to you alone: a class, a trip, a long-postponed project. One day you'll notice you went a whole afternoon without thinking about them, and that's how it ends, not with a decision but with a forgetting."""),

("what do i say to someone whose mom just died",
"""Say less than you think you should, and mean it. "I'm so sorry about your mom" is enough to begin. If you knew her, a specific memory is a gift: something she said, a kindness she did, the way she laughed. Avoid explanations and silver linings, like "she's in a better place" or "at least she isn't suffering," which tend to land as attempts to close the subject. Then offer practical help that doesn't require them to think: bring food, run an errand, sit with them. And keep checking in after the funeral. The weeks afterward, when everyone else has gone back to normal, are often the hardest."""),

("i made a mistake at work that cost the company money",
"""Own it quickly and plainly. Tell your manager before they hear it from someone else: what happened, what it cost as far as you know, and what you've already done to contain it. Then bring a proposal for preventing it from happening again, such as a checklist, a review step, or an alert. Managers are generally far more forgiving of mistakes than of concealment, and someone who handles an error well often ends up more trusted than before. It's also worth remembering that a mistake that cost money means you were trusted with something that mattered. Learn the lesson, then let the shame go."""),

("how do i stay motivated learning to code when i keep getting stuck",
"""Getting stuck isn't a sign you're failing at programming; it's most of what programming is. Experienced engineers are stuck constantly; they've simply learned to find it less alarming. Build a few habits for being stuck: read the error message carefully, slowly, word by word; explain the problem out loud to an imaginary listener; take a short walk; then search for the exact error. And work on something you genuinely want to exist, whether that's a small game, a tool for a hobby, or a website for a friend, because wanting the result pulls you through the frustrating middle in a way that tutorials can't."""),

("is 40 too old to learn piano",
"""No. Adults learn instruments more slowly than children in some ways and faster in others: you understand music theory more easily, practice more deliberately, and know why you want to play, which matters enormously. What you won't do is get much better without regular practice, so aim for twenty or thirty minutes most days rather than marathon weekends. A teacher, even occasionally, will save you from habits that are hard to unlearn. In five years you will be forty-five either way. The only question is whether you'll be forty-five and able to play."""),

("my neighbor's dog barks all night and i can't sleep",
"""Start with a friendly conversation, since there's a good chance your neighbor doesn't know; dogs often bark when their owners are asleep or away. Knock at a reasonable hour and keep it neighborly: "I don't know if you've noticed, but your dog has been barking a lot at night, and it's been keeping me up. Is there anything we can work out?" If you'd rather not knock, a polite note works. Give them time to deal with it, since training takes a while. If nothing changes after a couple of weeks, a landlord, a homeowners' association, or local noise rules are the next step, and you'll be in a stronger position for having tried kindness first."""),

("how do i deal with a coworker who talks way too much",
"""Set boundaries that are about your time rather than their personality. When they drop by, stay standing, or say early on, "I've got about five minutes before I need to get back to this." Headphones are a widely understood signal of focus. When a conversation needs to end, end it warmly and clearly: "I've enjoyed this, but I need to get back to work." Most talkative people aren't trying to be a burden; they simply haven't noticed the cues. Clear, kind signals usually work better than hoping they'll eventually take the hint."""),

("i just became a manager for the first time and i'm nervous",
"""Being nervous means you understand the job matters, which is a better starting point than overconfidence. In your first weeks, listen more than you change. Meet one-on-one with each person on your team and ask what's working, what isn't, and what they'd like from you. Set up regular one-on-ones and protect them. Your most important job now is helping other people do their best work, which means removing obstacles, clarifying priorities, and giving honest feedback early, while it's still small. Find a more experienced manager you can talk to, and expect to make mistakes. Admitting them openly will earn you more trust than pretending you haven't."""),

("how do i handle criticism without getting defensive",
"""Give yourself a gap between hearing it and responding to it. In the moment, your only job is to understand, so ask questions like "Can you give me an example?" or "What would it look like if I did it better?" rather than explaining yourself. Then thank them, and say you'll think about it. Later, when the sting has faded, sort the criticism honestly: what's accurate, what's partly accurate, and what doesn't fit. Even unfair criticism often contains one useful grain. Over time, you'll notice the defensiveness arriving and passing more quickly, and people will trust you more for it."""),

("i want to quit my job but i'm scared",
"""Fear is a reasonable response to a big change, and it doesn't necessarily mean you're making the wrong decision. It helps to make the fear specific. Is it money? Then work out how many months of expenses you have saved, and how many you'd need to feel safe. Is it uncertainty about what comes next? Then start exploring while you still have the job: talk to people, apply quietly, take on a small project. Is it what others will think? That one usually shrinks when you look at it directly. A good exit is rarely a dramatic leap; it's usually a series of small, deliberate steps that eventually make leaving feel obvious."""),

("should i lend money to a friend",
"""Only lend an amount you could afford to lose without resenting it. Money between friends has a way of changing the friendship, and an unpaid debt can sit between you for years. If you decide to help, it can be cleaner to treat it as a gift, or at least to agree that it's a gift if they can't repay it. If it's a true loan, be clear about the amount and the timeline, and write it down, not because you distrust them but because memories drift. And if you need to say no, you can do it kindly: "I can't lend money, but I'd like to help in another way." Friends usually understand that more than you'd expect."""),

("my 5 year old refuses to eat vegetables",
"""This is normal, and it usually passes with patience rather than battles. Children often need to encounter a new food ten or more times before they'll accept it, so keep putting small amounts on the plate without pressure, bribes, or negotiations. Eat vegetables yourself where they can see you. Involve them: let them choose a vegetable at the store, wash it, or stir it into something. Serve vegetables alongside foods they already like, and in different forms, since a child who rejects steamed carrots may love them roasted. The goal for now isn't clean plates; it's familiarity, which over time becomes acceptance."""),

("i took a sick day and i feel guilty about it",
"""Rest when you're unwell isn't something you got away with; it's the purpose of a sick day. Working while sick tends to make you slower and recover more slowly, and it puts your colleagues at risk if you're contagious. The guilt is mostly habit, the residue of workplaces that made people feel their presence was proof of their worth. Your team will manage for a day. Give yourself permission to actually rest, which means not checking email every hour. You'll come back better able to do the work that's waiting."""),

("how do i reconnect with an old friend i haven't talked to in years",
"""Just reach out, simply and without apology for the silence. Something like "I was thinking about you the other day, remembering when we drove to the coast and the car broke down. How have you been?" works well, because a specific memory shows that you're reaching out to them, not just to anyone. Don't overthink the gap; most people are delighted to hear from an old friend and assume the drift was mutual, because it usually was. If they reply warmly, suggest a call or coffee. Some reconnections bloom and some stay small, and both are worth having."""),

("i hate my job and someone at a party asked what i do. how do i answer that without being a downer",
"""Answer briefly and steer toward something you actually like talking about. "I work in insurance claims. It pays the bills. Lately I've been much more interested in the pottery class I'm taking" gives them a fact and a better thread to pull. People ask what you do mostly as a way of starting a conversation, not to evaluate your career, so you're free to redirect it toward whatever you'd enjoy discussing. You might find that being asked often enough nudges you toward a job you'd be happy to describe."""),

("how do i stop overthinking every text i send",
"""Notice that the person on the other end is almost certainly reading your message far less carefully than you're writing it. Most texts are skimmed in a few seconds between other things. Try writing what you mean in a natural first draft, reading it once for tone, and then sending it, without a fourth revision. If you're worried about how something will land, it often helps to say what you mean more directly rather than more delicately. And when you catch yourself replaying a message you've already sent, remind yourself that you can't edit it now, and that if something needs clarifying, the other person will usually ask."""),
]
